Host-staged all-reduce for two GPUs without peer-to-peer access (fork).

Two consumer cards without NVLink (2x RTX 3090 on PCIe 4.0 x16) have no P2P, so
custom all-reduce is off and NCCL runs the tensor-parallel all-reduce through
host shared memory with SM-driven copies at ~3.7 GiB/s. A 2048-token prefill
chunk does 128 all-reduces of 20 MiB (hidden 5120, bf16): 5.3 ms each, ~45% of
the prefill, and TP=2 prefilled no faster than one card (8k: 1,338 tok/s on two,
1,350 on one).

This backend maps one shared, pinned host buffer into both ranks and lets the
copy engines move the data: each rank sends the peer's half of its contribution,
adds the peer's contribution to its own half and sends the reduced half back,
1x the tensor out and 1x in per rank. 20 MiB take 1.83 ms against NCCL's 5.23
(bit-identical result). Cross-rank ordering uses 64-bit sequence flags in the
shared buffer, written by a one-thread st.release.sys kernel and awaited by a
one-thread ld.relaxed.sys polling kernel, compiled once with NVRTC to a cubin
(containers often ship no PTX JIT). Found on the way: cuStreamWriteValue64 on an
idle stream lands ~2 ms late, cuStreamWaitValue64 polls with a millisecond
backoff and a stream blocked on a host semaphore stalls the device's other
streams, and a volatile load of host memory can be served from L2 -- each of
these alone turned 1.8 ms into 7 ms.

Only large, eager all-reduces use it (VLLM_ALLREDUCE_HOST_STAGED_MIN_KB ..
_MAX_MB, default 1 MiB .. 32 MiB, never inside a CUDA-graph capture); decode
stays on PyNCCL. Opt-in: VLLM_ALLREDUCE_HOST_STAGED=1 (the single-user launcher
sets it at --tensor-parallel-size 2).

Measured (2x RTX 3090, TP=2, single-user dflash2, warm): 8k prefill 1,338 ->
1,691 tok/s, 32k 1,063 -> 1,241 on the Triton int8-KV profile.

